COVID-19 Pneumonia
Respiratory System
Dry cough/Fever, Silent hypoxia, Peripheral ground-glass opacities

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RPS-014
Reason for Visit: Persistent fever, dry cough, and sudden profound weakness with low oxygen readings on a home monitor, requiring urgent clinical assessment.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a patient with known viral symptoms now recording oxygen saturations in the low 80s despite not feeling severely breathless. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to evaluate for "silent hypoxia" and initiate immediate medical intervention before the regular in-hours clinic opened.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I feel completely exhausted, I have lost my sense of taste, and my home oxygen monitor keeps flashing red even though I don't feel that short of breath.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms started 5 days ago with fever, generalized myalgias, anosmia, and ageusia. Over the past 48 hours, the patient developed a dry, persistent cough and profound fatigue and malaise. Today, the patient noted gastrointestinal symptoms (diarrhea) and pleuritic chest pain. Despite feeling only mildly breathless, their home pulse oximeter shows profound hypoxia ("silent hypoxia"). The dyspnea is entirely out of proportion to the objective lack of overt respiratory distress.
Functional Status: Usually independent; currently confined to bed due to extreme lethargy and weakness.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; isolated due to infection control protocols.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 115/70 mmHg | HR: 112 bpm | Temp: 38.5 C | RR: 24 breaths/min | SpO2: 84% on room air | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Appears fatigued and flushed, but conversational. Lacks the expected severe respiratory distress given the degree of hypoxemia.
Physical Exam: Chest auscultation reveals bilateral fine crackles predominantly in the peripheral and basilar lung fields. Cardiovascular exam demonstrates marked tachycardia. Patient is able to speak in full sentences despite SpO2 of 84%, classic for COVID-19 related silent hypoxia.
Environmental Observations: House is closed up; poor ventilation.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking regular paracetamol with only transient effect on fever.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: COVID-19 Pneumonia - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Pulmonary Embolism - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Presence of fever, myalgias, anosmia, and bilateral fine crackles.

Asthma Exacerbation - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of wheeze, presence of bilateral crackles, and lack of bronchodilator response.

Typical Bacterial Pneumonia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Dry cough rather than purulent sputum, and peripheral bilateral crackles rather than unilateral focal consolidation.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is exhibiting the hallmark signs of COVID-19 pneumonia, specifically profound "silent hypoxia," persistent dry cough, fever, and bilateral peripheral crackles. The discrepancy between the patient's perceived comfort and their critical oxygen saturation (84%) is highly dangerous. Deferring assessment until the next in-hours period would predictably result in sudden cardiovascular collapse or anoxic brain injury. An urgent after-hours home visit was absolutely necessary to verify the hypoxemia, institute immediate oxygen therapy, administer systemic corticosteroids, and arrange controlled transfer to a respiratory ward.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Initiated continuous oxygen therapy at 4L/min via nasal prongs, improving SpO2 to 92%. Administered oral Dexamethasone 6mg immediately. Arranged urgent ambulance transfer to the COVID-19 receiving hospital.
Positive Behavioral Change: Lie flat on your stomach (proning) for 30 minutes right now; this specific position immediately uses gravity to pull blood into the healthier parts of your lungs, directly resulting in higher oxygen levels in your blood.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not applicable; hospital admission required.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Handover provided to the Emergency Department admitting team.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Patient is being transferred to the Emergency Department immediately due to critical hypoxemia requiring inpatient monitoring and antiviral therapy.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The presumptive diagnosis of COVID-19 pneumonia requires confirmation via PCR testing and chest imaging in the hospital showing peripheral ground-glass opacities.
Clinical Course: The disease course is unpredictable; the profound fatigue and lung inflammation require hospital-level support to manage the immune response and oxygen requirements safely.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Keep your oxygen mask firmly in place during the ambulance ride; removing it even briefly will cause your oxygen levels to drop to critically dangerous levels immediately.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The virus has caused widespread inflammation in the tiny air sacs at the edges of your lungs, preventing oxygen from crossing into your blood; the steroids act to shut down this excessive inflammation.
Actionable Steps: Practice taking slow, deep breaths in through your nose and out through your mouth; controlling your breathing rate directly reduces the energy your body spends, preserving your remaining oxygen reserves.

